  "parameters": [],
  "explanation": "Joins returns with products to get the category, filters for returns in 2022, and sums the refund amounts grouped by category."
}

{
  "sql": "SELECT p.product_name, (SUM(CASE WHEN DATE_PART('year', r.return_date) = 2022 THEN r.return_quantity ELSE 0 END) * 1.0 / NULLIF(SUM(CASE WHEN DATE_PART('year', o.order_date) = 2022 THEN oi.quantity ELSE 0 END), 0)) AS return_ratio FROM commerce.products AS p JOIN commerce.order_items AS oi ON p.product_id = oi.product_id JOIN commerce.orders AS o ON oi.order_id = o.order_id LEFT JOIN commerce.returns AS r ON oi.order_id = r.order_id AND p.product_id = r.product_id GROUP BY p.product_name ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 200",
  "parameters": [],
  "explanation": "This query calculates the ratio of returned units to ordered units for each product in 2022. It joins products with order items and orders to get the ordered quantities. It left joins returns to capture returned quantities, filtering both by the year 2022. The ratio is calculated as the sum of returned quantities divided by the sum of ordered quantities. The results are ordered by this ratio in descending order to find the products with the highest return rates."
}

{
  "clarification": "The request to calculate the ratio of returned units to ordered units for 2022 is ambiguous because the 'returns' table does not have an approved join to the 'order_items' table (only to 'orders' and 'products'). This prevents accurately attributing returned quantities to specific order line items (which contain the 'quantity' for ordered units) without risking double-counting or incorrect grain alignment. Additionally, the definition of 'ordered units' (total quantity in order_items vs. distinct orders) and 'returned units' (sum of return_quantity) needs clarification to ensure the ratio is calculated on a consistent grain."
}

